{
  "what": "Three arms under policies v2 and v3 on dev and val, and the headline (P14, D29).",
  "rule": "Lowest pooled (dev + val) error rate among accepted fields; among the arm-policy pairs within 0.1 percentage points of the lowest, the lower pooled review rate; if still tied, the lower pooled cost per page. Fixed before the comparison ran. Arm B is reported regardless, as the all-open-weights configuration.",
  "caveats": [
    "dev and val are both development data (D26). Nothing here was scored on the frozen test set.",
    "Cost per page: the extraction run's recorded spend plus every verification call (occlusion and re-reads, second looks included) at the price it was first made at, cache hits counted. Muse Spark 1.3 runs at Contributor prices (D19); Muse Spark Open's are the figures Baseten's pricing page prints (config/models.toml), not a contract.",
    "Pooled intervals resample the pages of dev and val together. The extractor of B differs from A and C, so B's fields carry different extraction errors: its error rate measures the whole configuration, not the verifier alone.",
    "baseline_error_rate_accepted is the error rate of the extraction run alone, which accepts every value it didn't abstain on.",
    "The P14 run was resumed once. At --workers 8, 538 Baseten re-reads of arms B and C failed on HTTP 429 after their retries, and a failed re-read sends its field to review; those scores are kept in arms_compare_first_pass.json. A failed call is not cached, so the resume (--workers 2) asked only those calls again and replayed every answer already given; no re-read or second look of B or C failed in the end. The rule chose the same winner on both passes. Arm A's two failed re-reads on val are empty answers, not errors, and stand."
  ],
